::: {.doc-subject}

Check if the graph is strongly connected.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L81609-L81687){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.is_strongly_connected()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Is strongly connected](../assets/graphs/is-strongly-connected.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: two directed triangles joined by edges in both directions between C and D, so every node can reach every other along the arrows and is_strongly_connected() returns True. Right: the same graph without the edge from D back to C (drawn dashed in the accent color); nothing in D, E, F can reach A, B, C, so is_strongly_connected() returns False." width="100%"}

A directed graph is strongly connected if there is a directed path from every node to every
other node. Equivalently, the graph has exactly one strongly connected component containing
all nodes.

For undirected graphs, this is equivalent to checking regular connectivity (same as
`is_connected()`).
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if the graph is strongly connected, `False` otherwise. Empty graphs are
    considered strongly connected (vacuously true).
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

Returns `True` iff the entire node set forms a single SCC, delegating to the same
Kosaraju pass as `strongly_connected_components()` and short-circuiting once a
second component is discovered, so the effective cost is $O(V + E)$. Empty graphs
are treated as trivially strongly connected, matching the vacuously-true convention
that pairs of nonexistent nodes need no path. On undirected graphs strong and
ordinary connectivity coincide, so this call falls through to `is_connected()`
semantics.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~strongly_connected_components`) 

:   The components of a graph that is not.

[](`~is_semiconnected`) 

:   The looser check, with one node of each pair reaching the other.

[](`~is_weakly_connected`) 

:   The check that ignores edge directions.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

In a directed cycle, every node can reach every other by following the edges around. A
node that can reach the cycle, but that nothing reaches, breaks strong connectivity:

::: {#55c8f325 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

cycle = [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "A")]
graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(cycle, directed=True)
before = graph.is_strongly_connected()

graph.add_node(label="D")
graph.add_edge("D", "A")

before, graph.is_strongly_connected()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


On an undirected graph, this is the same as being connected:

::: {#789c3415 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
ec.Graph().add_path_graph(4).is_strongly_connected()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
True
```
:::
:::


### A circle of accounts

`payments()` is a month of payments between 18 accounts at a made-up bank, each directed
from payer to payee. The whole month isn't strongly connected, since many accounts are
never paid back. But the eight accounts that pass money around among themselves are,
taken on their own:

::: {#a5492fee .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
payments = ec.payments()
circle = max(payments.strongly_connected_components(), key=len)
ring = payments.subgraph(sorted(circle))

payments.is_strongly_connected(), ring.is_strongly_connected()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
(False, True)
```
:::
:::


::: {#6826b70d .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
ring.plot(label_by="label", curved_edges=True, seed=3)
```

